text:
zoe: Quick pass through the Next.js starter for a new hire onboarding today. Found three small but painful DX issues: env var in .env.example is NEXT_PUBLIC_REDWOOD_API_KEY (client) but server code expects REDWOOD_API_KEY, Python LlamaIndex sample uses old generate() surface, and our eval harness CI flakes because streaming is enabled by default. Repro + suggested fixes below.

omar: ugh, can you paste the minimal repro for the 401?

zoe: Yep. Clone, cp .env.example .env, npm run dev, call /api/complete. Server route reads process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY so it ends up undefined if user copied NEXT_PUBLIC_ var and you get a 401. Minimal snippet: 
```js
// pages/api/complete.js
export default async function handler(req, res) {
  const key = process.env.REDWOOD_API_KEY; // undefined for users who only copied NEXT_PUBLIC_ var
  const r = await fetch('https://api.redwood.ai/v1/responses', {
    method: 'POST',
    headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${key}` },
    body: JSON.stringify({ input: req.body.text })
  });
  res.status(r.status).send(await r.text());
}
```

lucy: that's a common newbie trap. Fix: change .env.example to REDWOOD_API_KEY and add a clear comment: "SERVER-ONLY: do not prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_." Also add a one-line "first-run checklist" in README.

omar: re: SDK parity: the Python LlamaIndex example still calls generate(...) while JS samples use responses.create. People flip between languages and get confused. Suggest adding a tiny compat shim in samples to map generate -> responses.create if available. Example shim: 
```py
# samples/compat.py
from redwood import responses

def create_compat(*args, **kwargs):
  return getattr(responses, 'create', responses.generate)(*args, **kwargs)
```

zoe: love that. Also for onboarding, include a short note in LangChain/LlamaIndex READMEs listing SDK versions -> method mapping (generate -> responses.create) and a one-line pip/npm pin suggestion.

lucy: about the eval harness: CI fails intermittently because some models stream tokens and snapshot tests expect full strings. Quick mitigation: add a --capture-mode flag (values: stream|final) and default CI to "final". Also add a single retry on transient network/model timeouts.

omar: and add a tiny withRetries util in the JS sample repo, but explicitly warn not to retry on 401 by default (that hides auth problems). Example: 
```ts
// samples/utils/withRetries.ts
export async function withRetries(fn, retries = 2) {
  let i = 0
  while (true) {
    try { return await fn() }
    catch (e) {
      if (e?.status === 401 || i++ >= retries) throw e
      await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 150 * i))
    }
  }
}
```

ci-bot: Created issue #517 in https://github.com/redwood-inference/samples: "nextjs starter: env var exposure + SDK parity + eval streaming flakes" assigned to @omar.

zoe: I'll bundle a PR that: 1) updates .env.example -> REDWOOD_API_KEY with server-only note, 2) adds first-run checklist to README, 3) adds JS withRetries util and docs caveat, 4) adds python compat shim for responses.create/generate, 5) adds --capture-mode to eval harness and flips CI to final.

lucy: please also add one small test for the harness that runs with --capture-mode final to prevent regressions.

omar: PR opened: https://github.com/redwood-inference/samples/pull/658  tagging @lucy and @zoe for review. Thanks all for quick triage.

zoe: great, these fixes should make first-run much smoother. leaving a short doc blurb: "If you see 401 on first-run: check .env for REDWOOD_API_KEY (server-only) and avoid exposing NEXT_PUBLIC_ secrets)." :white_check_mark:
